bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor.mem:85:''A RAISE'S EXPIRY IS ITS NEXT CAUSAL BOUNDARY'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation; <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/rulings">>). An ember's raise lives one boot and lapses at the first of a lock, a restart, a human unlock or a counted removal; that boundary satisfies the expiry this carrier requires, and the custody ruling's //non-renewal// reads as it. A raise fenced on a Nexus's lease epoch lapses at the causal event that rolls the frontier past it (#/the-breaks). No timer and no heartbeat ends a standing: a quiet ember, a quiet peer and a quiet clock each leave a raise standing until a boundary arrives. An ember's raise spends its count at the raise ledger's durable commit, before SERVING composes: a crash before the commit spends nothing and serves nothing, and a crash after it burns that raise. ''The residue, stated plainly'': a hand that writes an older raise ledger onto a disk copy restores spent raises, and no count detects it (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/ember#/the-raise">>).
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor.mem:210:''AN EPOCH FENCES PER NEXUS, and one frontier gives the raise BOTH properties it needs.'' A raise binds the Nexus's lease epoch: ''supersession'' (a newer grant binds a higher epoch) and ''expiry'' (an old one goes stale when the frontier rolls past it), both driven by the Nexus's own events rather than by a duration anybody must agree on, with no clock anywhere (#/the-raise-is-a-vessel-layer-act, where the rolling window of markers stands STRUCK). A vessel standing in more than one Nexus holds more than one frontier, and ''an epoch means nothing outside the Nexus that rolls it''.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor.mem:256:''Supersession and expiry come from that one mechanism, with no clock anywhere.'' A newer grant binds a higher epoch; an old one goes stale when the frontier rolls past it. A paused, forwarded or replayed raise self-revokes at the next roll — no global broadcast, no duration anybody must agree on, and a returning holder stays fenced whether or not anyone suspected it. This matters most for the vessel this law governs: ''a vessel AT THE FLOOR holds no trustworthy wall time at all''.
